 "7e8868266f10e1de": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Example grammar",
    [
     "E → E + E | E * E | id  with * above + and both left-associative."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Operator precedence relation table",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "a \\ b",
        "id",
        "+",
        "*",
        "$"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "id",
         "",
         "⋗",
         "⋗",
         "⋗"
        ],
        [
         "+",
         "⋖",
         "⋗",
         "⋖",
         "⋗"
        ],
        [
         "*",
         "⋖",
         "⋗",
         "⋗",
         "⋗"
        ],
        [
         "$",
         "⋖",
         "⋖",
         "⋖",
         ""
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "Blank cells (id id, $ $) are errors: two operands in a row, or empty input."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Operator precedence function table",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "",
        "id",
        "+",
        "*",
        "$"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "f",
         "4",
         "2",
         "4",
         "0"
        ],
        [
         "g",
         "5",
         "1",
         "3",
         "0"
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "a ⋖ b ⇔ f(a) < g(b);  a ⋗ b ⇔ f(a) > g(b);  a ≐ b ⇔ f(a) = g(b)."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Comparison",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Point",
        "Relation table",
        "Function table"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "Storage",
         "n × n entries (n terminals)",
         "2n integers"
        ],
        [
         "Lookup",
         "index the matrix with (a, b)",
         "compare two numbers f(a), g(b)"
        ],
        [
         "Error detection",
         "Blank entries flag errors immediately (e.g. id id)",
         "Every pair compares, so blank entries are lost; errors are found later, during reduction"
        ],
        [
         "Construction",
         "Directly from LEADING/TRAILING or precedence rules",
         "From the relation table via the f/g graph; fails if the graph has a cycle"
        ],
        [
         "Generality",
         "Always possible for an operator-precedence grammar",
         "Not every relation table has precedence functions"
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "Example: input id id — the matrix gives blank ⇒ error at once; the functions give f(id) = 4 > g(id) = 5 ⇒ ⋗, so the parser reduces and only later finds no matching handle."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 2 notes, p. operator-precedence section — LEADING/TRAILING, relation table, precedence functions"
  ]
 },
