 "b3fe07b8afe931cc": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "(i) FIRST, FOLLOW and the table",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Non-terminal",
        "FIRST",
        "FOLLOW"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "R",
         "{ #, (, a, b }",
         "{ #, %, (, ), a, b, $ }"
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "Table rule: for A → α, put the production in M[A, a] for every a in FIRST(α); if ε ∈ FIRST(α), also in M[A, b] for every b in FOLLOW(A).",
     "The grammar is LL(1) exactly when no cell gets two productions.",
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "NT",
        "#",
        "%",
        "(",
        ")",
        "a",
        "b",
        "$"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "R",
         "R → R # / R → # R / R → R R / R → R %",
         "",
         "R → R # / R → R R / R → R % / R → ( R )",
         "",
         "R → R # / R → R R / R → R % / R → a",
         "R → R # / R → R R / R → R % / R → b",
         ""
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "Multiply-defined entries: M[R, b] = R → R # / R → R R / R → R % / R → b; M[R, a] = R → R # / R → R R / R → R % / R → a; M[R, (] = R → R # / R → R R / R → R % / R → ( R ); M[R, #] = R → R # / R → # R / R → R R / R → R % ⇒ NOT LL(1)."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "(ii) Justification — NOT LL(1)",
    [
     "Left recursion: R → R#, R → RR, R → R% start with R, so FIRST(R#) = FIRST(R) = {#, (, a, b}. These clash with R → #R, R → (R), R → a and R → b in the same cells.",
     "Ambiguity: e.g. “#a#” = (#a)# or #(a#), and “aaa” = (aa)a or a(aa). An ambiguous grammar can never be LL(1) (or LR(1)).",
     "Every cell of row R has several productions, so a predictive parser cannot decide with one lookahead."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 2 notes, p. LL(1) / predictive parsing section — FIRST, FOLLOW, table construction"
  ]
 },
