 "196c5857c7a0bd4f": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Optimizations found",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Code",
        "Technique",
        "Where / why"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "a = 10, b = 20, c = 30",
         "Constant propagation",
         "values known everywhere below"
        ],
        [
         "x = a + b + 5;  y = a + b + 5;",
         "Common subexpression + folding",
         "y = x; both = 35"
        ],
        [
         "z = c * 2",
         "Constant folding",
         "z = 60"
        ],
        [
         "int unusedVar = 100, temp = x + y",
         "Dead-code elimination",
         "never used"
        ],
        [
         "arr[i] + c * 2 in the loop",
         "Loop-invariant code motion / folding",
         "c*2 = 60 computed once"
        ],
        [
         "x = x + i * 4",
         "Induction variable & strength reduction",
         "t += 4 instead of i*4 (or fold: x = 35 + 4·(0+1+2+3+4) = 75)"
        ],
        [
         "the 5-iteration loop",
         "Loop unrolling",
         "constant trip count; arr becomes {61, 62, 63, 64, 65}"
        ],
        [
         "int p = x",
         "Copy propagation",
         "p replaced by x (= 75)"
        ],
        [
         "if (p > 50)",
         "Constant folding / branch elimination",
         "75 > 50 is true: y = 35 + 75 = 110; else-branch is dead"
        ],
        [
         "computeSum(arr, 5)",
         "Inlining + folding",
         "61 + 62 + 63 + 64 + 65 = 315"
        ]
       ]
      }
     }
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Optimized code (keeping the array work explicit)",
    [
     "int computeSum(int arr[], int n) {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int *p = arr, *e = arr + n; p < e; p++)   // pointer induction variable\n        sum += *p;\n    return sum;\n}\nint main() {\n    int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) arr[i] += 60;     // c*2 hoisted and folded\n    int x = 75, y = 110, z = 60;\n    int total = computeSum(arr, 5);\n    printf(\"x=%d, y=%d, z=%d, total=%d\\n\", x, y, z, total);\n    return 0;\n}"
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Fully folded version",
    [
     "int main() {\n    printf(\"x=%d, y=%d, z=%d, total=%d\\n\", 75, 110, 60, 315);\n    return 0;\n}"
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 5 notes, p. 137–144, 213–215 — principal sources of optimization"
  ]
 },
 "f53390a2ee5f3ac4": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Role",
    [
     "The code generator turns the optimized intermediate representation into target code. Its output must be correct, use the machine’s resources well, and the generator itself should run efficiently. Finding optimal code is undecidable in general, so heuristics are used."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Issues",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Issue",
        "What the code generator must decide",
        "Example of its effect"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "1. Input to the code generator",
         "IR form (TAC, quadruples, DAG, postfix) plus symbol-table info; input assumed type-checked and error-free",
         "Knowing x is float makes it emit ADDF rather than ADD"
        ],
        [
         "2. Target program",
         "Absolute machine code, relocatable object code or assembly",
         "Relocatable code allows separate compilation and linking"
        ],
        [
         "3. Memory management",
         "Map names to addresses (static, stack offset, heap); resolve labels to addresses",
         "A local i becomes 8(SP); a goto L becomes a jump to an actual address after backpatching"
        ],
        [
         "4. Instruction selection",
         "Choose instruction sequences; exploit special instructions and addressing modes; costs matter",
         "a = a + 1 → INC a (cost 3) instead of MOV a,R0; ADD #1,R0; MOV R0,a (cost 6)"
        ],
        [
         "5. Register allocation & assignment",
         "Which values live in registers, and which register each gets; even/odd pairs for some ops",
         "Keeping a loop counter in a register saves a load and a store every iteration"
        ],
        [
         "6. Evaluation order",
         "Order of computing subexpressions affects how many registers are needed",
         "t1=a+b; t2=c+d; t3=e−t2; t4=t1−t3 needs more registers than computing e−(c+d) first"
        ]
       ]
      }
     }
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Illustrations",
    [
     "Instruction selection: for a = b + c; d = a + e a statement-by-statement generator reloads a just after storing it.",
     "; naive, statement by statement           ; better\nMOV  b, R0                                 MOV  b, R0\nADD  c, R0                                 ADD  c, R0\nMOV  R0, a          ; a = b + c            ADD  e, R0\nMOV  a, R0          ; redundant reload     MOV  R0, d\nADD  e, R0\nMOV  R0, d          ; d = a + e",
     "Register allocation with pairs: on machines like the IBM 370, M x, y needs an even/odd register pair (x in the even register, product in the pair); DIV leaves remainder in the even and quotient in the odd register — the allocator must respect this.",
     "Evaluation order: for (a + b) − (e − (c + d)), computing e − (c + d) first needs 2 registers; left-to-right order needs 3.",
     "Memory management: static data at fixed addresses (MOV x, R0), stack data at offsets from SP (MOV 12(SP), R0); jump targets are resolved after code is laid out."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 6 notes, p. 5–15 — issues in the design of a code generator",
   "Compiler Design Module 6 notes, p. 16–22 — target machine and instruction costs"
  ]
 },
 "56aba7a3f37b4858": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Allocation vs assignment",
    [
     "Register allocation decides WHICH values should be kept in registers at each point; register assignment decides WHICH physical register each one gets."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Approaches",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Approach",
        "Strategy",
        "Effect on code"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "Local (per basic block)",
         "Use next-use information: getreg puts an operand in a register that already holds it, else an empty one, else spills the value whose next use is farthest",
         "Simple and fast; values are stored at block boundaries"
        ],
        [
         "Global, usage counts",
         "Benefit of keeping x in a register for loop L ≈ Σ over blocks (use(x, B) + 2·live(x, B)); give registers to the highest-benefit variables",
         "Big gains for loop variables; ignores non-loop code"
        ],
        [
         "Fixed (reserved) registers",
         "Dedicate registers to stack pointer, base pointers, return values",
         "Predictable, but fewer registers left"
        ],
        [
         "Graph colouring (Chaitin)",
         "Build the interference graph (edge if two values are live together); colour it with k colours; spill when simplification gets stuck",
         "Near-optimal allocation across a whole procedure; costly to compute"
        ],
        [
         "Linear scan",
         "Sort live intervals by start; assign registers greedily, spill the interval ending last",
         "Fast (used by JIT compilers) with slightly worse code"
        ],
        [
         "Outer-loop / hierarchical",
         "Allocate innermost loops first, then propagate to enclosing loops",
         "Keeps hot values in registers where it matters most"
        ]
       ]
      }
     }
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Comparison and evaluation",
    [
     "Local allocation is cheap but reloads values at every block boundary. Usage-count global allocation targets loops and removes most memory traffic there. Graph colouring gives the fewest spills over a procedure and handles live ranges precisely, at higher compile-time cost. Linear scan trades a little code quality for compile speed. Fewer loads/stores means fewer cycles and smaller code, which is why register allocation is usually the most profitable back-end optimization."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 6 notes, p. 29–30, 37–39 — register allocation and assignment, graph colouring",
   "Compiler Design Module 6 notes, p. 27 — next-use information"
  ]
 },
