 "d08606a2e89261ad": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Role",
    [
     "The code generator turns the optimized intermediate representation into target code. Its output must be correct, use the machine’s resources well, and the generator itself should run efficiently. Finding optimal code is undecidable in general, so heuristics are used."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Issues",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Issue",
        "What the code generator must decide",
        "Example of its effect"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "1. Input to the code generator",
         "IR form (TAC, quadruples, DAG, postfix) plus symbol-table info; input assumed type-checked and error-free",
         "Knowing x is float makes it emit ADDF rather than ADD"
        ],
        [
         "2. Target program",
         "Absolute machine code, relocatable object code or assembly",
         "Relocatable code allows separate compilation and linking"
        ],
        [
         "3. Memory management",
         "Map names to addresses (static, stack offset, heap); resolve labels to addresses",
         "A local i becomes 8(SP); a goto L becomes a jump to an actual address after backpatching"
        ],
        [
         "4. Instruction selection",
         "Choose instruction sequences; exploit special instructions and addressing modes; costs matter",
         "a = a + 1 → INC a (cost 3) instead of MOV a,R0; ADD #1,R0; MOV R0,a (cost 6)"
        ],
        [
         "5. Register allocation & assignment",
         "Which values live in registers, and which register each gets; even/odd pairs for some ops",
         "Keeping a loop counter in a register saves a load and a store every iteration"
        ],
        [
         "6. Evaluation order",
         "Order of computing subexpressions affects how many registers are needed",
         "t1=a+b; t2=c+d; t3=e−t2; t4=t1−t3 needs more registers than computing e−(c+d) first"
        ]
       ]
      }
     }
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Illustrations",
    [
     "Instruction selection: for a = b + c; d = a + e a statement-by-statement generator reloads a just after storing it.",
     "; naive, statement by statement           ; better\nMOV  b, R0                                 MOV  b, R0\nADD  c, R0                                 ADD  c, R0\nMOV  R0, a          ; a = b + c            ADD  e, R0\nMOV  a, R0          ; redundant reload     MOV  R0, d\nADD  e, R0\nMOV  R0, d          ; d = a + e",
     "Register allocation with pairs: on machines like the IBM 370, M x, y needs an even/odd register pair (x in the even register, product in the pair); DIV leaves remainder in the even and quotient in the odd register — the allocator must respect this.",
     "Evaluation order: for (a + b) − (e − (c + d)), computing e − (c + d) first needs 2 registers; left-to-right order needs 3.",
     "Memory management: static data at fixed addresses (MOV x, R0), stack data at offsets from SP (MOV 12(SP), R0); jump targets are resolved after code is laid out."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 6 notes, p. 5–15 — issues in the design of a code generator",
   "Compiler Design Module 6 notes, p. 16–22 — target machine and instruction costs"
  ]
 },
 "2cc019992475d516": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Answer",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Error",
        "Detected in",
        "Why not earlier"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "i) x = y + z;  (z not declared)",
         "Semantic analysis",
         "‘z’ is a valid identifier lexeme and “id = id + id ;” is grammatically correct. Only the symbol-table lookup during semantic analysis finds that z has no declaration."
        ],
        [
         "ii) x = y ** 5;  (invalid operator)",
         "Syntax analysis",
         "The scanner happily produces two valid tokens * and * (there is no ** token in C). The parser then finds operator-operator with no operand between them — no grammar rule matches. (In real C, * * 5 parses as y * (*5) and the semantic analyzer rejects dereferencing an int.)"
        ],
        [
         "iii) if (a + b > c) x = 10  (missing ;)",
         "Syntax analysis",
         "All tokens are valid; only the parser, which expects ‘;’ to end the statement, can see that it is missing."
        ],
        [
         "iv) int x = \"hello\";  (type mismatch)",
         "Semantic analysis (type checking)",
         "Tokens are valid and “type id = expr ;” is a valid declaration. The mismatch int ← char* is found only when types are compared."
        ],
        [
         "v) a = b = c = 5;  (no chained assignment)",
         "Syntax analysis",
         "If the grammar is stmt → id = expr ; and expr cannot contain =, the parser fails at the second ‘=’. The scanner sees only valid ids, = and a number."
        ]
       ]
      }
     }
    ]
   ],
   [
    "Rule of thumb",
    [
     "Lexical: illegal characters, bad numbers, unterminated strings. Syntax: wrong token order (missing ;, unbalanced brackets). Semantic: undeclared names, type mismatch, wrong argument count. A phase can only see the information it works on, so each error is caught by the first phase that has the needed information."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 1 notes, p. 99 — error handling in each phase",
   "Compiler Design Module 1 notes, p. 119 — error handling"
  ]
 },
 "3ef1c1288f5b629a": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "Lex program",
    [
     "%{\n#include <stdio.h>\nint digits = 0, alphabets = 0, specials = 0;\n%}\n\n%%\n[0-9]        { digits++; }\n[a-zA-Z]     { alphabets++; }\n[ \\t]        { /* ignore blanks */ }\n\\n           { return 0; }          /* end of the input line */\n.            { specials++; }\n%%\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Enter the input: \");\n    yylex();\n    if (digits)    printf(\"Contains digits (%d)\\n\", digits);\n    if (alphabets) printf(\"Contains alphabets (%d)\\n\", alphabets);\n    if (specials)  printf(\"Contains special characters (%d)\\n\", specials);\n    return 0;\n}\nint yywrap(void) { return 1; }"
    ]
   ],
   [
    "How it works",
    [
     "Section 1 (%{ … %}) declares the counters. Section 2 has pattern–action rules; Lex tries the patterns in order and picks the longest match, so ‘.’ (any other character) catches only what is not a digit, letter or blank.",
     "yylex() runs the generated DFA over the input and executes the action of each matched rule.",
     "Run: lex prog.l → cc lex.yy.c → ./a.out.  Input “ab#12” → Contains digits (2), alphabets (2), special characters (1)."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 1 notes, p. 46–47 — Lex – lexical analyzer generator"
  ]
 },
