 "4f2c460ea6759182": {
  "sections": [
   [
    "a. Why the serial application is no longer adequate",
    [
     "1. Workload growth: smaller grid cells mean many more cells (halving the cell size in 3-D gives 8× the cells, plus smaller time steps), but the program still uses one core — 30 hours vs a 6-hour limit.",
     "2. It is serial legacy code: no parallel constructs, poorly documented, original developers gone — it cannot use the many cores of modern machines, and rewriting 500,000 lines by hand is risky."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "b. Why a faster single-core CPU will not help",
    [
     "Clock speeds have stopped rising (power/heat wall); a realistic faster core gives perhaps 1.2–1.5×, while 30 h → 6 h needs 5×. Memory bandwidth and latency also limit a single core on large grids. Performance today comes from more cores, not faster ones."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "c. Best candidate for parallelization",
    [
     "The nested loops over the 3-D grid that update each cell’s heat transfer, fluid dynamics and pressure for a time step. Each cell’s new value depends only on values from the previous step (its neighbours), so the cell updates within one step are independent — massive data parallelism, and these loops take almost all the run time."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "d. Automatic parallelization of such a loop",
    [
     "! original Fortran loop\nDO k = 1, nz\n  DO j = 1, ny\n    DO i = 1, nx\n      Tnew(i,j,k) = T(i,j,k) + c*(T(i+1,j,k)+T(i-1,j,k)+T(i,j+1,k) &\n                    + T(i,j-1,k)+T(i,j,k+1)+T(i,j,k-1) - 6*T(i,j,k))\n    END DO\n  END DO\nEND DO\n\n! what the parallelizing compiler effectively produces\n!$OMP PARALLEL DO PRIVATE(i, j)\nDO k = 1, nz\n  …same body…\nEND DO",
     "The compiler: (1) builds the dependence graph — Tnew is written and T only read, so there is no loop-carried dependence; (2) checks that i, j are private loop indices; (3) marks the outer k loop DOALL and splits its iterations among threads; (4) vectorizes the inner i loop; (5) inserts a barrier at the end of the time step."
    ]
   ],
   [
    "e. Risks of automatic parallelization on legacy code",
    [
     {
      "table": {
       "head": [
        "Risk",
        "Impact"
       ],
       "rows": [
        [
         "Wrong dependence assumptions (aliasing, COMMON blocks, EQUIVALENCE, globals)",
         "If the compiler is too optimistic → race conditions and wrong forecasts; too conservative → loops stay serial, little speed-up"
        ],
        [
         "Floating-point reductions reordered",
         "sums computed in a different order give slightly different results — forecasts not bit-reproducible"
        ],
        [
         "Serial fraction (I/O, setup)",
         "Amdahl: with 95% parallel code the maximum speed-up is 20×; reaching 5× needs ≥ 7 cores (1 / (0.05 + 0.95/7) ≈ 5.4)"
        ],
        [
         "Overheads: thread start-up, synchronisation, load imbalance, false sharing",
         "can make small loops slower than serial"
        ],
        [
         "Unstructured code (GOTOs, I/O inside loops, calls with side effects)",
         "blocks parallelization of exactly the loops that matter"
        ]
       ]
      }
     },
     "Mitigation: validate parallel output against the serial version, add hints (OpenMP directives, restrict), and refactor hot loops guided by profiling."
    ]
   ]
  ],
  "sources": [
   "Compiler Design Module 7 notes, p. 1–17, 35–41, 72–81 — parallelization and automatic parallelization"
  ]
 },
